\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}
\label{sec:why_kstar}
\label{sec:one_wash}
\label{sec:negative_graduated}
\label{sec:repr_dependence}
\label{sec:boundary_cases}
\label{sec:length_confound}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_jsd_decay.pdf}
\caption{Token-level JSD between adjacent depth pairs on log scale. Blue band: min-max range across 8 C4 models (1B--14B) with $\Kempirical{=}2$; dashed purple: Mistral-7B ($\Kempirical{=}1$, cross-scorer validated); green: arXiv Pythia ($\Kempirical{=}3$); dotted red: retrain-chain ($\Kempirical{=}7$). C4 models exhibit a sharp cliff ($\lambda \approx 2.2$--$3.5$), while arXiv decays gradually ($\lambda \approx 1.0$) and retrain-chain shows no cliff ($\lambda = 0.285$).}
\label{fig:jsd_decay}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{One-wash laundering (web text).}
For general web text, $\Kempirical{=}2$ reflects concentrated information loss: 0v1 is near-trivially separable ($\auc \geq 0.92$), 1v2 drops by ${\sim}$30\,pp, and 2v3 falls below $\theta$, so a single generation pass renders text indistinguishable from deeper content.
Neither frontier LLMs nor alternative classifiers improve depth recovery beyond this boundary (McNemar $p > 0.05$; Appendix).

\paragraph{JSD decay and the discrimination boundary.}
Token-level JSD between adjacent generations reveals two decay regimes (Figure~\ref{fig:jsd_decay}): C4 models exhibit a sharp cliff (0v1 JSD exceeds 1v2 by 6--56$\times$) followed by rapid convergence, while arXiv decays ${\sim}$3$\times$ slower ($\lambda \approx 1.0$ vs.\ $2.9$).
The retrain-chain ($\lambda = 0.285$, no cliff) reflects cumulative model-adaptation artifacts, extending $\Kempirical$ to 7 (Appendix~\ref{sec:jsd_extended}).

\paragraph{Graduated filtering failure.}
For web text, 2v3 $\auc < \theta$ across all configurations, so depth assignments beyond $d{=}2$ approximate random labels; the strategy ordering binary $>$ graduated aligns with $\Kempirical{=}2$ (Section~\ref{sec:filtering}).

\paragraph{Representation and scorer dependence.}
$\Kempirical$ is representation-dependent (frozen Qwen embeddings yield $\Kempirical^{\text{neural}} = 3$ vs.\ $\Kempirical^{\text{neural}} = 2$ for Pythia and OLMo; Table~\ref{tab:neural_probe_crossmodel}) and scorer-dependent (cross-family scoring reduces Wikipedia $\Kempirical$ from 2 to 1, Appendix~\ref{sec:wiki_domain}, while preserving C4 feature rankings $\rho > 0.90$ and $\Kempirical$ for 6/8 configurations, Section~\ref{sec:transfer}).

\paragraph{Domain dependence of $\Kempirical$.}
C4 yields $\Kempirical{=}2$ (8/9 configurations), Python code $\Kempirical{=}2$ for four models across four architecture families ($\Kempirical{=}1$ for capacity-limited Pythia-1.4B; Section~\ref{sec:cross_domain}), arXiv $\Kempirical{=}3$ (five models, four families), and Wikipedia $\Kempirical{=}1$ (Appendix~\ref{sec:wiki_domain}); the ordering correlates with structural complexity (Table~\ref{tab:domain-ttr-entropy}).
Code and C4 share $\Kempirical{=}2$ but with shifted cliff positions: syntactic constraints preserve depth-1 signal in code, so domain structure governs both $\Kempirical$ and the decay profile.
The only C4 exception (Mistral-7B, $\Kempirical{=}1$) yields $\Kempirical{=}2$ on code and $\Kempirical{=}3$ on arXiv; a LaTeX ablation confirms content rather than formatting drives the arXiv difference (Appendix~\ref{sec:latex_ablation}).

\paragraph{Surprisal attenuation and boundary variation.}
\label{sec:surprisal_attenuation}
The Mistral-7B exception ($\Kempirical{=}1$) arises from compressed surprisal dynamic range (Table~\ref{tab:cohens_d_full}); larger 0$\to$1 surprisal drops correlate with higher 1v2 $\auc$ (Table~\ref{tab:borderline_features}), predicting $\Kempirical$ may decrease beyond 14B.

\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related_work}

\paragraph{Model collapse and recursive degradation.}
Iterative training on model outputs causes tail-distribution degradation~\cite{shumailov2024collapse, alemohammad2024selfconsuming, dohmatob2024model, dohmatob2025strong}, diversity contraction~\cite{guo2024curious, padmakumar2024writing, briesch2023selfconsuming}, and predictable convergence~\cite{bertrand2024stability, schaeffer2025position, kazdan2025collapse, gerstgrasser2024model, shi2025collapse}.
\citet{gambetta2024surplexity} propose surplexity, a single surprisal-based collapse indicator; our 15-dimensional feature set captures richer distributional structure and enables cross-model transfer.
\citet{amin2025escaping} show that weak-data curation can mitigate collapse, connecting to our filtering evaluation (Section~\ref{sec:filtering}).
Our prompt-chain operates in the replace regime~\cite{kazdan2025collapse}; $\Kempirical = 2$ is therefore a conservative lower bound.
This literature establishes convergence but provides no per-text depth measurement, does not predict domain dependence of saturation (Wikipedia depth 1 vs.\ arXiv depth 3), and does not characterize the prompt-chain vs.\ retrain-chain gap ($\Kempirical{=}2$ vs.\ $7$).

\paragraph{Beyond binary detection.}
Binary detectors~\cite{gehrmann2019gltr, mitchell2023detectgpt, bao2024fast, yang2024dnagpt, hans2024binoculars, verma2024ghostbuster, basani2026diveye, zhang2024minkpp} achieve strong human-vs.-machine separation but produce no depth estimate; surveys~\cite{wu2023survey, tang2024science} consolidate this landscape but frame detection as binary.
Recent work extends beyond binary labels to collaboration degree~\cite{cheng2025beyond}, contamination hierarchies~\cite{mehta2025beyond}, and multigenerator benchmarks~\cite{wang2024semeval}; robustness concerns arise under paraphrasing~\cite{sadasivan2023detect, krishna2023paraphrasing} and cross-mode transfer~\cite{hao2025rewrite}; CoDeC~\cite{zawalski2026codec} addresses contamination via membership inference.
None estimates recursive generation depth.
Watermarking~\cite{kirchenbauer2023watermark} embeds detectable signals at generation time but does not apply to text already in the wild.

\paragraph{Data selection and curation.}
DSIR~\cite{xie2023dsir}, D4~\cite{tirumala2023d4}, DoReMi~\cite{xie2023doremi}, and FineWeb~\cite{penedo2024fineweb} address corpus quality for large web corpora such as C4~\cite{raffel2020t5} through domain-aware filtering and deduplication but are depth-agnostic; Drayson et al.~\cite{drayson2025} combined binary detection with importance resampling, a special case when $\Kstar = 1$.
\citet{oren2024proving} address contamination detection in pre-training data; our method extends this to depth-granular resolution.

\paragraph{Ordinal classification and probing.}
Our surprisal features build on information-theoretic processing cost~\cite{hale2001surprisal, levy2008expectation}, repurposed to track generational degradation.
Our OBD classifier follows Frank \& Hall's~\cite{frank2001ordinal} binary decomposition; CORAL~\cite{cao2020coral} provides a rank-consistent neural alternative.
Our neural probe extends probing~\cite{alain2017understanding, belinkov2022probing} to the discrimination boundary.

